          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Iron deficiency anemia sits in the blood chapter. Which chapter would an underlying bleeding ulcer be coded from?",
          "answer": "A bleeding peptic ulcer would be coded from the digestive system chapter, Chapter 11 of ICD-10-CM (codes K00 to K95). The chapter summary places the peptic ulcer categories, divided by site (gastric, duodenal, peptic unspecified and gastrojejunal), in the esophagus, stomach and duodenum block at K20 to K31. (Source: coding-icd10-chapter-11-digestive.)\n\nThe blood chapter note you mentioned also fits this. It says that chronic blood loss causing iron deficiency is coded from whichever chapter owns the bleeding site. So the iron deficiency anemia would sit in Chapter 3, and an underlying ulcer that is bleeding would sit in Chapter 11. (Source: coding-icd10-chapter-03-blood.)\n\nThe search results did not include the specific ulcer codes or the rules for coding bleeding within them, so I cannot confirm the exact code for a bleeding ulcer. A medical coder or the official ICD-10-CM tables would give that detail. This is general coding information, not a statement about any person's health. If you are worried about bleeding, such as black or bloody stools, vomiting blood, or feeling faint, seek medical care promptly.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
